// `unparseExpr` renders a failing condition back to kama source, and returns "" for a form it does not
// cover — a documented degrade to a bare "assertion failed". That contract only ever held at the TOP of
// the expression: a composite arm spliced an unrendered CHILD straight into its output, so an uncovered
// subexpression left a HOLE in the middle of the text (` == 6`) rather than degrading. A hole reads as a
// compiler bug, and it hides which half of the condition the reader should be looking at.
//
// A ternary is the cheapest form the renderer does not cover, and it is a stand-in: the point is the
// degrade, not this operator. If a `?:` arm is ever added, keep the fixture and pick another.
fn int32 main() {
    comptime assert(cond: (2 > 1 ? 5 : 6) == 6, msg: "a condition the renderer cannot spell");
    return 0;
}
